#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167679} :activity/id #uuid "631d41d0-caaf-5a9a-8598-3ad89711698e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048884 :topic/id #uuid "c96d046b-16b8-5aad-81a5-2dd906b25702" :topic/math-academy-id 3269} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168077 :tutorial/content "Recall that a discrete uniform distribution is a probability distribution that models the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mid S \\mid$ is the size (cardinality) of $S$.\n\nDiscrete uniform distributions occur regularly in games of chance.\n\nFor example, suppose that in a particular lottery, players must select a number from the following set:\n\n$$\n\\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}\n$$\n\nLet's assume that a player randomly selects a number from this set such that each number has an equal probability of being selected. Then, if $X$ represents the number the player chooses, we can model this situation using a discrete uniform distribution, where\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}\n$$\n\nFurther, since\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2,\\ldots,50\\}\n$$\n\nhas\n\n$$\n\\mid S \\mid = 50\n$$\n\nitems, the probability mass function for the random variable $X$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{50},\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ {1, 2, …, 50} \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c576c848-4a58-54d4-a011-7e6c489c8225" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5101 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "79d64508-f3b9-51ba-b040-3e3b55ece653" :step/math-academy-id 32013 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167769}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166838 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166680 :question/id #uuid "dad3d0a2-26e0-52c4-9e5a-abe6664cc02c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0e2ce6a-ee66-50a0-88b4-47dc0189ff51" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167514}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling a Situation in Context With the Discrete Uniform Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "1cf23694-2167-55c4-9d0e-c4724313b90c" :step/math-academy-id 32014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166687 :question/id #uuid "de4ae41e-c981-5b5e-b52c-c8e7caf4c272" :question/math-academy-id "e-8647"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00b14560-29f9-58fc-ac65-5505987579fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048404}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167516}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Situations That Can Be Modeled as a Discrete Uniform Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "832deb31-bd88-5bbe-acc1-91719627f9fd" :step/math-academy-id 32093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168134 :tutorial/content "To apply a discrete uniform probability model to a situation in context, there are two things we need to check for:\n\n- The support $S$ of our random variable should be *finite*, and\n- each $x\\in S$ must be *equally likely*.\n\nThere are some common pitfalls that we need to be aware of!\n\n- First, we cannot use a discrete uniform distribution to model continuous phenomena.For example, weight, time, and distance are all quantities that can take *any* real number within a given interval. Such quantities should be modeled as continuous, so a discrete uniform distribution is usually inappropriate.\n- Second, we must be wary of discrete phenomena requiring infinitely large support.For example, suppose $X$ represents the number of road accidents at a particular junction in a given month. Now, while $X$ is (hopefully) a very small number, it could theoretically be *any* positive integer for modeling purposes! For this reason, a variable with infinite support is more appropriate. Moreover, $X$ is unlikely to be uniformly distributed since having $X=2$ accidents per month is more likely than $X=1\\,000\\,000$." :tutorial/id #uuid "aabc2db5-2a28-567d-94ed-1b215e353ccc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6457 :tutorial/title "Determining When a Discrete Uniform Distribution is Appropriate"} :step/id #uuid "1abe1dd3-86a6-56dd-9bbf-f7a1402c711c" :step/math-academy-id 39012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167684}}] :activity/title "Modeling With Discrete Uniform Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167033}]}
